From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] shell32, explorer: the Recycle Bin as Windows 10 has it -- Empty Recycle Bin, its Properties, per drive; This PC and the user's folder Properties; the address bar's working folder

GitHub #22, #21, #20. The bin could only be emptied by selecting everything in
it and choosing Erase; the desktop's Recycle Bin, This PC and the user's folder
did nothing on Properties; the bin's icon was always the full one.

Empty Recycle Bin, as Windows has it:
- on the bin's own menu (the desktop icon's, any view of the desktop
  namespace), greyed while the bin holds nothing; the bin's menu has no Cut,
  Copy or Delete; in the bin's folder, on its background menu (before
  Properties) and on File Explorer's command bar ("Empty Recycle Bin",
  "Restore all items" / "Restore the selected items", shown in the bin only);
- SHEmptyRecycleBin asks "Delete File" / "Delete Folder" / "Delete Multiple
  Items": "Are you sure you want to permanently delete these N items?" (No
  keeps them; nothing is asked when there is nothing to empty), honours
  SHERB_NOCONFIRMATION and SHERB_NOSOUND (the scheme's EmptyRecycleBin sound),
  empties one drive's bin when given a path on it;
- an item goes for good with all it holds: DeleteFileW alone left every deleted
  folder in the bin.

Per user and per drive: what Wine deletes still goes to the user's own Trash
(in the home); what a Linux program deletes on another disk lies in that
disk's Trash ($topdir/.Trash-$uid or .Trash/$uid). The bin shows, counts,
restores and empties both; only Trash folders that belong to the caller's own
account are read. SHQueryRecycleBin counts one drive's bin or all, and a
deleted folder's size is what it holds (the Size column too).

The bin's own menu starts with Open (bold, its default; a shell folder with no
verbs of its own had none, and Send to was the default).

The bin's icon is the "Empty" or "Full" value of its DefaultIcon (the user's
own HKCU\...\Explorer\CLSID\{bin}\DefaultIcon first), as the bin is; the
desktop watches the user's Trash and reads its icons again when the bin fills
or empties (a delete in any program, Empty, a Linux program). shell32 had no
icon at -32 at all: the empty bin is trash_file.ico there now, as Windows has
the empty bin at shell32,-32.

Properties:
- the Recycle Bin: its own sheet ("Recycle Bin Properties"): the locations,
  one per drive with its size; for the one selected "Custom size: Maximum size
  (MB)" or "Don't move files to the Recycle Bin. Remove files immediately when
  deleted."; "Display delete confirmation dialog". Kept where Windows 10 keeps
  them: HKCU\...\Explorer\BitBucket\Volume\{volume GUID} MaxCapacity and
  NukeOnDelete (the drive letter names it where a drive has no volume GUID),
  SHELLSTATE's fNoConfirmRecycle (SHGetSetSettings now sets it; a
  ConfirmFileDelete policy checks and greys it). And honoured: a drive set not
  to recycle deletes for good, asked as such (Windows looks at the first
  item's drive); the oldest items of a drive's bin go for good to make room
  for a new one; an item bigger than the whole bin is "too big to recycle.
  Do you want to permanently delete it?"
- This PC: Settings > About (ms-settings:about), as Windows 10's This PC >
  Properties; the user's folder and any shell item with a folder: that
  folder's sheet; a folder's background Properties: the folder's sheet
  (FIXME before); SHObjectProperties takes the bin's parsing name.

File Explorer's address bar: a program typed there starts in the folder shown,
as on Windows ("cmd" opens there, "notepad file.txt" finds the file); and a
program with its arguments runs, as the Run box takes it (the quoted part, else
the shortest leading part up to a space that names a program): "notepad
file.txt" said "File Explorer can't find". The user's own folder (the desktop
icon with the user's name) opens at C:\users\<name>, not an empty page.

Gate: make test-recyclebin.
